  ["The only muscle of the soft palate not supplied by the vagus (pharyngeal plexus) is:","Tensor veli palatini",["Levator veli palatini","Musculus uvulae","Palatopharyngeus"],"Tensor veli palatini is supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve through the nerve to medial pterygoid; the others are vagal."],
  ["On saying 'ah', a patient's uvula deviates to the right. The most likely lesion is of the:","Left vagus nerve",["Right vagus nerve","Left hypoglossal nerve","Right glossopharyngeal nerve"],"The healthy right levator pulls the palate up and toward itself, so the uvula points away from the weak left side; the opposite rule applies to the tongue, which points toward the lesion."],
  ["A 24-year-old has numbness and loss of taste on the right side of the tongue after removal of 4.8. The structure injured is the:","Right lingual nerve",["Right inferior alveolar nerve","Right hypoglossal nerve","Right chorda tympani in the middle ear"],"The lingual nerve lies against the lingual plate at the third molar and carries both general sensation and the chorda tympani taste fibres; inferior alveolar injury would numb the lip and chin instead."],
  ["A 65-year-old smoker with a carcinoma at the base of the tongue complains of pain in the ipsilateral ear with a normal ear examination. The referred pain travels through the:","Glossopharyngeal nerve, supplying both tongue base and middle ear",["Lingual nerve, which supplies the external ear canal and tragus","Facial nerve, which carries general sensation from the base of the tongue","Hypoglossal nerve, which supplies sensation to the auricle"],"CN IX supplies the posterior third of the tongue and, through its tympanic branch, the middle ear, so tongue-base pain is misread as ear pain; the lingual nerve does not reach the ear, and the facial nerve carries taste, not general sensation, from the tongue."],
  ["The most numerous lingual papillae, which lack taste buds, are the:","Filiform papillae",["Fungiform papillae","Circumvallate papillae","Foliate papillae"],"Filiform papillae are keratinised, give the tongue its rough texture and contain no taste buds; the other three types carry taste buds."],
  ["The palatal mucosa over the roots of 1.1 to 1.3 receives its sensory supply from the:","Nasopalatine nerve",["Greater palatine nerve","Lesser palatine nerve","Anterior superior alveolar nerve"],"The nasopalatine nerve exits the incisive foramen and supplies the palate from canine to canine; the greater palatine nerve begins behind the canines."],
  ["The pterygomandibular raphe is a tendinous band joining the:","Buccinator and the superior pharyngeal constrictor",["Masseter and the medial pterygoid at the angle","Temporalis and the buccinator","Superior and middle pharyngeal constrictors"],"Buccinator arises from the raphe anteriorly and the superior constrictor from it posteriorly; the raphe is the medial landmark for the inferior alveolar nerve block."],
  ["In the gag reflex, the afferent and efferent limbs are respectively carried by the:","Glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves",["Vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves","Lingual and hypoglossal nerves","Trigeminal and facial nerves"],"Touching the oropharynx is sensed by CN IX and the pharyngeal and palatal muscles contract through CN X; the trigeminal and facial nerves are not part of the arc."],
  ["A 12-year-old has a painless midline neck swelling that rises when the tongue is protruded. The swelling is most likely a:","Thyroglossal duct cyst",["Ranula","Dermoid cyst of the floor of the mouth","Submental lymph node"],"The thyroglossal duct runs from the foramen caecum of the tongue to the thyroid, so a cyst along it is tethered to the tongue and moves with protrusion; a ranula lies in the floor of the mouth, not the neck."],
  ["During surgery in the floor of the mouth, the lingual artery is found by:","Dividing or retracting hyoglossus, deep to which the artery runs",["Following the lingual nerve, which lies deep to hyoglossus","Tracing the hypoglossal nerve, which lies deep to hyoglossus","Retracting genioglossus, which covers the artery laterally"],"Hyoglossus is the key: the lingual artery passes deep (medial) to it while the lingual and hypoglossal nerves pass superficial (lateral) to it."]
