 understand: `
 <p><b>Every injection is an anatomy problem: solution must bathe the nerve.</b> Thin, porous maxillary bone lets solution placed in the buccal fold at a tooth apex (<b>supraperiosteal infiltration</b>) reach the nerve; the thick adult mandibular cortex does not, so the nerve is blocked before entering bone.</p>
 <p><b>Inferior alveolar nerve block (IANB).</b> The target is the nerve at the mandibular foramen, in the <b>pterygomandibular space</b>. Three landmarks: the <b>coronoid notch</b> (deepest point of the anterior ramus border), the <b>pterygomandibular raphe</b> medially, and the lower <b>occlusal plane</b>. The needle enters from the opposite premolars, about 6 to 10 mm above the occlusal plane, lateral to the raphe, and touches bone at roughly 20 to 25 mm, where the needle is withdrawn about 1 mm and solution is deposited after aspiration. Bone met very early means the tip is too far anterior: withdraw and swing the barrel over the front teeth. No bone at full depth means the tip is too far posterior, in the parotid, where solution paralyses the facial nerve. The lingual nerve is caught on withdrawal; the long buccal nerve needs its own deposit distal to the last molar. The IANB has the highest positive aspiration rate of common injections, because the inferior alveolar vessels run with the nerve (Malamed: about 10 to 15 percent). In children the foramen lies below the occlusal plane, so inject lower.</p>
 <p><b>Gow-Gates</b> blocks the whole mandibular division at the <b>lateral neck of the condyle</b>: mouth wide open, barrel aligned from mouth corner to intertragic notch, intraoral target distal to the mesiolingual cusp of the maxillary second molar. It usually covers the buccal nerve, with a low aspiration rate but slower onset. <b>Vazirani-Akinosi</b> is the closed-mouth block for trismus: needle parallel to the maxillary occlusal plane at the mucogingival junction of the upper molars, advanced about 25 mm with no bone contact.</p>
 <p><b>Mental and incisive blocks</b> use the <b>mental foramen</b>, usually between and below the premolar apices (4.4/4.5). The mental block numbs lip, chin and buccal gingiva; finger pressure after deposit pushes solution into the canal to reach premolar and anterior pulps (incisive block).</p>
 <p><b>Posterior superior alveolar (PSA)</b>: needle in the buccal fold above the second molar, angled upward, inward and backward along the tuberosity; overinsertion punctures the <b>pterygoid plexus</b> and causes a rapid cheek haematoma. The PSA often misses the mesiobuccal root of the first molar, served by the <b>middle superior alveolar</b> nerve when present (about 28 percent of patients, per Malamed). <b>Infraorbital (ASA)</b>: the foramen lies below the infraorbital notch in line with the pupil and second premolar; the needle runs up the fold over the first premolar toward a finger on the foramen; pressure then numbs incisors to premolars, lower eyelid, nose and upper lip.</p>
 <p><b>Palatal injections.</b> The <b>greater palatine foramen</b> lies at the junction of alveolar process and palate opposite the second or third molar; a small deposit numbs the palatal mucosa from the premolars back; too far posteriorly numbs the soft palate and provokes gagging. The <b>nasopalatine block</b> enters beside the incisive papilla and numbs the palate from canine to canine on both sides; it is the most painful injection, so pressure anaesthesia and a slow, tiny volume are used.</p>
 <p><b>Why the exam cares.</b> Stems name a technique and ask its landmark, or describe a complication (haematoma, facial palsy, trismus, numb tongue) and ask which injection caused it.</p>`,
